  <h2 id="causes">What Causes a Blackout?</h2>
  <p>When a person drinks alcohol to the point of intoxication, they can temporarily block the transfer of memories from short-term to long-term storage in the hippocampus in the brain. The process being blocked is called memory consolidation, and it can happen even while a person maintains skills such as talking.</p>
  <p>There's also research that shows specific regions of the brain are selectively vulnerable to the effects of alcohol. For example, alcohol-related blackouts can alter development in brain regions associated with visual memory. They can also cause retrograde memory impairment, where a person experiences blackouts because of gaps in memory consolidation and because of being unable to retrieve memories in long-term storage.</p>

  <h2 id="susceptible">Who Is Susceptible to Blackouts?</h2>
  <p>Anyone can experience a blackout when drinking alcohol. Around one third of adults who drink alcohol have had a blackout in the past year, while one half of college students have had an alcohol-related blackout.</p>
  <p>However, there are environmental and genetic factors that can increase the likelihood of someone having a blackout while drinking.</p>
  <p><b>Environmental factors include:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Drinking alcohol without eating before</li>
    <li>Drinking quickly</li>
    <li>Binge drinking</li>
    <li>Drinking while taking medications</li>
    <li>Experiencing blackouts in the past</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Genetic factors include:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Having parents who experience blackouts</li>
    <li>Family members with an addiction</li>
    <li>Alcohol-metabolizing genes that change how the body processes alcohol</li>
    <li>Central nervous system and hippocampal sensitivity to high blood alcohol concentrations</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Women are more likely to have blackouts than men. This disparity is because, on average, women weigh less than men and have less water in their bodies, so they reach peak blood alcohol concentration faster. In fact, one study found women who consume just one more drink than their usual amount are 13% more likely to blackout.</p>

  <h2 id="risks">What Are the Risks of Blacking Out?</h2>
  <p>Blacking out carries many risks. The immediate one is the increased risk of injury: people who have experienced six or more blackouts are more than 2.5 times more likely to experience an alcohol-related injury.</p>
  <p>Blackouts can also increase the risk of poor decision-making and dangerous behavior, such as driving while intoxicated. On a social level, memory impairment can damage relationships.</p>
  <p>Longer-term risks are also present. One study revealed that frequent blackouts are significantly related to more memory lapses and more problems with everyday cognitive functioning.</p>
  <p>Other studies noted the psychiatric effects of blackouts. Blackouts have been associated with suicidal ideation and suicide attempts, plus can be predictive of major depression among people with an alcohol use disorder (AUD).</p>

  <h2 id="treatment">Treatment for Alcohol Abuse</h2>
  <p>Having blackouts from alcohol doesn't automatically mean that a person has an alcohol use disorder, but it can be a sign that something isn't right. AUD can be slow and quiet to manifest — it often starts with small justifications for drinking and easy dismissal of the seriousness of blackouts.</p>
  <p>Treatment for alcohol abuse helps people safely withdraw from alcohol and acknowledge any effects that may have developed as a result of misusing alcohol. Professional treatment significantly improves safety and long-term recovery outcomes, particularly for individuals with moderate to severe alcohol dependence.</p>
  <p>Treatment spans <a href="/programs/medical-detox/">detox</a>, <a href="/programs/residential-treatment/">residential</a>, outpatient, and aftercare programs, the majority of which integrate <a href="/programs/medication-assisted-treatment/">Medication-Assisted Treatment (MAT)</a> to alleviate severe withdrawal symptoms, along with evidence-based therapies such as Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) to address the root causes of excessive alcohol use.</p>
  <p>Blackouts shouldn't leave anyone trying to recover in the dark. Treatment for <a href="/what-we-treat/alcohol/">alcohol addiction</a> is available and can be life-changing.</p>

